Z||000003||Fformula88||07-13-2001||05:11 PM||||Hey, wait a second!  Give us some more info!  I have recently done the swap on my Formula (which is what the link goes to).  It isn't very hard at all.  Since you see some rivets, I am assuming your existing steering wheel is a 3-spoke wheel.  This makes things easier since you have everything your going to need.<P>Step one, you do have to get rid of the rivets you see.  Use a drill bit slightly larger than the center and drill those suckers out, but be careful.  They are holding the fron and back plastic covers together so if the drill strays its going to go thru that plastic quickly.<P>Once its out, you will see the steering wheel is attached to the hub by allen screws.  Just remove them and you can attach the new wheel to the hub.  When you go to re-attach the plastic covers around the hub, forget using rivets and just get yourself some small bolts.  If you get a couple with flushmount phillips heads, you can use a nutdriver on the back and screw them tight.  Pup it back on the column, put the horn button back on, and your good to go.  <P>If you (or anybody else) has any questions or wants more details (sorry, I didn't think to take pics as I did it) email me  <A HREF="mailto:Fformula88@aol.com">Fformula88@aol.com</A>  or post back here.  Also IM me if I am online (AOL IM Fformula88).  Good luck!<P><P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierosig2.JPG"> <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ed||07-14-2001||01:09 AM||||Well, I just posted on another steering wheel thread, and here's this one.  So there are rivets!  Does that mean you don't use the puller?  And does it mean you don't have to mess with the cancellation tower?  And does it mean all three shop manuals I looked at, including the "official" one, are all wrong?<P>The world wants to know!<P>Fformula88, I'd like to know!  Its late and I'm going to the Fiero Fest in San Francisco tomorrow (without my new wheel).  The reason being related in my old thread THUMPED. (car crashed)  I'll e-mail you Sat. or Sunday, or please leave more info here.  And now I'm off to the thread suggested by our Dad. <P>P.S.  I just got back from that thread, which only made things more confusing.  Oh, well, tomorrow all should be revealed when 50 or so Fiero owners get together.  <p>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 07-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fformula88||07-14-2001||10:54 AM||||Sorry about the confusion Ed.  Its a little hard to explain without being able to point to it, but I'll try.<P>You will have to pull the wheel with a puller to do this job.  It may be possible to do it without one, but if you have a puller it makes it a lot easier.<P>You do NOT have to do anything to the cancelation tower.  <P>The rivets you see do NOT pose a big problem, but they do need to be removed.  Going from the outermost to the innermost parts on the steering hub, you have<P>Horn button<P>the plastic ring where you see the rivets.  <P>Under that is the steering wheel ring (like on the new one).  <P>Under that is the metal hub that the wheel attaches to (and where the whole thing mounts to the column)<P>And finally a plastic shield (an inch or two long) that hides all the inner stuff... it looks like the column and is the same diameter, but turns with the wheel).<P>Steps...<BR>1. Pull the wheel off the column with a puller.<P>2. Those rivets only attach the outer plastic ring (behind the horn button) and this last plastic shield together.  They go thru the metal components, but do not hold them on.  Using a small drill bit, drill them out and these two plastic pieces can be removed.  They drilled easily for me, so don't be afraid of them.  Also, they don't need to be replaced with rivets as long as the wheel is pulled off the car.<P>3. Now you will see some torx screws (I said allen before, but now remember they are torx... those ones that look like stars).  These hold the wheel to the hub.  Take them out and attach your new wheel.<P>4.  Now you need to re-attach those plastic trim parts.  Get some small bolts that have phillips screw ends that let the bolt sink (Not sure the technical name, but when installed they will be flat.  Put that end thru the trim ring on the front, and attach the nut on the back side inside of the large plastic shield.  They will do the same job as the rivets did.  You could use rivets again, but I didn't have any long ones, and figure the bolts would be easier to take apart if I ever did the job again.<P>5.  Re-attach wheel and enjoy!<P>Hope this helps a little... if you still have ?'s shoot me an email and I can try to explain more.  Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ed||07-14-2001||10:22 PM||||Good explanation, Fformula88, especially for me because now I've seen "the works."<P>Just got back from the west coast Fiero Fest in the San Francisco, Ca Bay area.  "Dave" from southern Ca was there with his redone wheels (check with him if you need one, he's on this board) and of course he also explained what was going on. His have the "works" all attached: the hub, plastic ring, etc. Its interesting that three shop manuals didn't talk about hubs and bare wheels and the rivets, etc.  I can see now its very easy and the wheel will be going on soon.<P>BUT, you don't have to twist and pull the horn contact (I earlier mistakenly called it the cancellation tower)?  Won't that get in the way?  Dave said to remove it.<P>Chris West was (is, its still going on), there with his 3.8 DOHC, turboed engine.  Pretty impressive.  <P>About two dozen cars in the judged portion of the show, with a few others choosing not to be judged.  One Mera, and the rest ranging from daily drivers to heavily trimmed out.   <p>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 07-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||07-15-2001||11:16 AM||||No problem Ed.  Good luck with the project!||||reg||1
